# Generated Kafka JSON event package (Java 17)

Each operation has a separate `__PACKAGE__.events.<Operation>Event` class, with
`send(runtime, payload, headers, key)` or `subscribe(runtime, groupOverride)`.
Shared payload/header/key records live in `__PACKAGE__.model`. KafkaCodec exposes
strict encode/decode functions for custom transports. There are no additional
raw/flat/idiomatic event styles yet.

Build with Maven: `mvn package`. Dependencies are pinned: official Apache Kafka
client 3.9.1, networknt JSON Schema validator 1.5.6, Jackson 2.18.3.
Provision topics separately; generation does not contact the broker.
`KafkaDefaults.connect()` uses configured brokers; `new KafkaRuntime("host:9092")`
replaces them. Close runtime and subscriptions explicitly.

Only plaintext Kafka, static topics, JSON payloads, closed nonnullable string
headers and optional nonnullable string keys are supported. Unsupported bindings,
security, schema dialects and model shapes fail generation. No schema registry,
tombstones, transactions, TLS/SASL or alternate brokers. Unknown formats are not
accepted. JSON Schema constraints validate before sending and after receipt.
UTF-8 is strict; duplicate JSON properties and Kafka headers are rejected.

Send returns `CompletionStage<RecordMetadata>` after broker acknowledgement.
The official producer enables idempotence and `acks=all`, with a 30-second
delivery deadline. Its transport retries do not deduplicate separate application
calls; Poolster adds no application retries. Cancelling the returned future does
not retract a record already sent. Operation-level client IDs select separate
producers; receive group/client IDs are honored.

Poll returns typed records with topic, partition and offset. Call `record.ack()`
only after processing succeeds, in partition order. Auto commit is disabled.
`consume(timeout, handler)` acknowledges only after a successful handler call;
exceptions stop the batch and leave remaining records uncommitted. Reopen the
same group to retry. Decode failure invalidates the subscription without
committing; close and reopen after handling the malformed message operationally.
Drain acknowledgements before polling another batch. Poll, ack and close run on
one thread; `cancel()` may be called elsewhere and wakes poll with Kafka's
`WakeupException`. Close on the consumer thread. Runtime close does not close
independent subscriptions. This is at-least-once processing, not exactly-once
application side effects.
